#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509545 :question/problem "Let $a = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, $b = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $(-2)a - (5b + 5c)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-2)a - (5b + 5c) &= (-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509548 :question/problem "Let $a = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, $b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $a - (5b + 5c)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - (5b + 5c) &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}40 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-37 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509551 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $5a-(b+5c).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-(b+5c) & =5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ -35\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ -35\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-34 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ -38\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509553 :question/problem "Let $a = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $a+3b$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na+3b &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509559 :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨ - 9, -4⟩$ and $b = ⟨11,13⟩$. Find $a - 2b$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $a - 2b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= ⟨-9,-4⟩ - 2\\cdot ⟨11,13⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-9,-4⟩ - ⟨22,26⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-9 - 22, -4 - 26⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-31,-30⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509563 :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨1, 13⟩$ and $b = ⟨1,-15⟩$. Find $a - 3b$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $a - 3b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨1,13⟩ - 3\\cdot ⟨1,-15⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,13⟩ - ⟨3,-45⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1 - 3, 13 - (-45)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-2,58⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509574 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨9,\\frac{27}{2}⟩$, then $2\\overset{\\to }{A}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $2\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{A} & =2\\cdot ⟨9,\\frac{27}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 9,2\\cdot \\frac{27}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,27⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509577 :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨14, -11⟩$ and $b = ⟨11,-8⟩$. Find $a - 2b$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $a - 2b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= ⟨14,-11⟩ - 2\\cdot ⟨11,-8⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨14,-11⟩ - ⟨22,-16⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨14 - 22, -11 - (-16)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-8,5⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509578 :question/problem "If $u = ⟨ - 11, -13⟩$ and $v = ⟨3,-1⟩$, then $u - v =$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $u - v$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu - v &= ⟨-11,-13⟩ - ⟨3,-1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-11 - 3, -13 - (-1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-14,-12⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509585 :question/problem "What is the sum of $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨14,-11⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{B}=⟨11,-8⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨14,-11⟩+⟨11,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨25,-19⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509596 :question/problem "What is the sum of $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨15,6⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{B}=⟨3,-4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨15,6⟩+⟨3,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509601 :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨15, 6⟩$ and $b = ⟨3,-4⟩$. Find $a + 2b$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $a + 2b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2b &= ⟨15,6⟩ + 2\\cdot ⟨3,-4⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨15,6⟩ + ⟨6,-8⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨15 + 6, 6 + (-8)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21,-2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509622 :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨14, -10⟩$ and $b = ⟨-14,5⟩$. Find $a - 3b$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $a - 3b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨14,-10⟩ - 3\\cdot ⟨-14,5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨14,-10⟩ - ⟨-42,15⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨14 - (-42), -10 - 15⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨56,-25⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509624 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨-4,- \\frac{7}{2}⟩$, then $-2\\overset{\\to }{A}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $-2\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-2\\cdot ⟨-4,- \\frac{7}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-2)\\cdot (-4),(-2)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509637 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨8,2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨-1,8⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =⟨8,2⟩+⟨-1,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8+(-1),2+8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509647 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨-5,-2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨-2,-1⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =⟨-5,-2⟩+⟨-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5+(-2),-2+(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509648 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{v} = ⟨-4,4⟩$, then $-4\\overset{\\to }{v} =$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\overset{\\to }{v} &= (-4)\\cdot ⟨-4,4⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨(-4)\\cdot (-4),(-4)\\cdot 4⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨16,-16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509658 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{U}=⟨6,1⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{V}=⟨-3,4⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V} & =⟨6,1⟩+⟨-3,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6+(-3),1+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509661 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{v} = ⟨8,-5⟩$, then $5\\overset{\\to }{v} =$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\overset{\\to }{v} &= 5\\cdot ⟨8,-5⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨5\\cdot 8,5\\cdot (-5)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨40,-25⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510365 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=4-4z$ at the point $(0,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =4-4z \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)k \\\\  & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j+4\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,2,2) & =2(0)\\,i-2(2)\\text{ }j+4\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,2,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,2,2)=⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510378 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+\\operatorname{cos}w \\\\ v \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}u+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+\\operatorname{cos}⁡w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡w.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510409 :question/problem "Find parametric equations for the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=1$ at the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).$ Then,  our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$  \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}) & =\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{e}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe parametric equations for the normal line to $f(x,y,z)$ at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ are given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={x}_{0}+{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ y & ={y}_{0}+{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ z & ={z}_{0}+{f}_{z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametic equations  for the normal line to our surface that  passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be written as \n \n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0,\\,z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510461 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 3v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y(u,v,w)=3v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510569 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{7}\\right)-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{56}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{280}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510573 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}-\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510575 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{7}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{27}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1784}{1323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510576 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{28}-\\left(-\\frac{21}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{513}{140}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510583 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{8}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{8}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{35}-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{113}{210}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510588 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(X)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -11 \\\\ -13 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(X) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & -11 \\\\ -13 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10\\cdot 7-(-11)\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =-70-143 \\\\  & =-213\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510602 :question/problem "What is $\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -9 \\\\ -7 & -10\\end{vmatrix}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -9 \\\\ -7 & -10\\end{vmatrix} & =-1\\cdot (-10)-(-9)\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =10-63 \\\\  & =-53\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510611 :question/problem "Calculate $det(R)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(R)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-8)(13)-(14)(-7) \\\\  & =-104+98 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510612 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7)(-15)-(-12)(7) \\\\  & =-105+84 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510613 :question/problem "Calculate $det(S)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(S)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-9)(9)-(1)(9) \\\\  & =-81-9 \\\\  & =-90.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510614 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-15 & -11 \\\\ 10 & -15\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-15 & -11 \\\\ 10 & -15\\end{vmatrix} & =-15\\cdot (-15)-(-11)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =225-(-110) \\\\  & =335\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510617 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-13 & 11 \\\\ 12 & -12\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-13 & 11 \\\\ 12 & -12\\end{vmatrix} & =-13\\cdot (-12)-11\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =156-132 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511457 :question/problem "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)=t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511504 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=6{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511542 :question/problem "![](images/86/86772e27f0a3226a608a1188fd8f58b9a8956f5f02fdbc5f9c2d29e2cef2a54c.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+12\\operatorname{cos}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}4t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511584 :question/problem "![](images/35/35b8ad6fd40daf95ba4f77288321d6a570e2df2944d056b9445e9ba4e36f97b7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{cos}3t,\\hspace{0.167em}3\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{sin}3t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}t+3\\operatorname{sin}3t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}t-3\\operatorname{cos}3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514383 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
